= Module 3: Rural-to-Urban Migration & Informal Slum Habitats

== 3.1 Push and Pull Dynamics of Distress Migration
Migration in India is a spatial reflection of profound regional socio-economic imbalances. While urban metropolises act as magnets of economic capital, technology, and commercial vitality, the rural agrarian hinterlands remain burdened by underemployment, low agricultural productivity, and lack of basic civic infrastructure.

#definition-box(title: "Push vs Pull Factors Driving Rural-Urban Migration")[
  - *Rural Push Factors (Expelling Dynamics):* Severe fragmentation of agricultural landholdings below economic viability; chronic agrarian indebtedness; monsoon droughts and flash floods; mechanization displacing landless agricultural laborers; lack of non-farm employment; and pervasive caste discrimination in traditional village social hierarchies.
  - *Urban Pull Factors (Attracting Dynamics):* Perceived higher monetary wage differentials; abundant employment opportunities in the informal unorganized sector (construction, street vending, domestic labor, freight transport); reliable electricity; and superior access to higher education, hospitals, and entertainment.
]

== 3.2 Genesis and Spatial Characteristics of Urban Slums
When millions of impoverished, unskilled rural migrants arrive in metropolitan cities, they encounter exorbitant urban land and rental markets governed by real estate speculation. Lacking financial resources to purchase or rent formal housing, migrants face severe *shelter poverty*.

Consequently, they are forced to illegally occupy marginal, precarious public lands devoid of commercial value—such as railway buffer tracks, river embankments, marshy drainage swales, and steep quarry hillslopes. These spontaneous squatter settlements expand into *slums (jhuggi-jhopris / chawls / bastis)*.

#important-box(title: "Salient Morphological Features of Indian Slums")[
  - *Extreme Spatial Overcrowding:* Multi-member families (often 6 to 10 persons) crammed into single dilapidated shanties measuring under 10 by 10 feet, constructed from flimsy tin sheets, asbestos, tarpaulin, and scrap wood.
  - *Severe Deficit of Water and Sanitation:* Absence of individual piped drinking water connections; complete lack of underground sewer systems; open roadside gutters carrying blackwater; and dependence on dysfunctional, unhygienic shared public community latrines.
  - *High Pathological Vulnerability:* Cramped, sunless living quarters without ventilation foster explosive transmission of airborne infectious diseases, predominantly *pulmonary tuberculosis*, alongside chronic gastrointestinal parasites and skin dermatoses.
  - *Informal Tenure Insecurity:* Constant threat of sudden municipal eviction and demolition creates socio-economic vulnerability, discouraging long-term household investment in physical dwelling improvements.
]

