== 3.3 Case Study: Dharavi (Mumbai) — Anatomy of an Informal Urban Hub
*Dharavi*, situated in the heart of Mumbai, is celebrated in human geography as one of the largest and most complex informal urban ecosystems in the world. It provides a striking empirical case study of how slum dwellers navigate structural marginalization through decentralized economic ingenuity.

#cheatsheet-box(title: "Geographical, Economic & Demographic Architecture of Dharavi")[
  - *Spatial Situation:* Sandwiched directly between the Western and Central suburban railway corridors of Mumbai, spanning an area of approximately *2.16 square kilometres*.
  - *Demographic Scale:* Inhabited by an estimated *700,000 to 1,000,000 people*, generating a staggering population density exceeding *300,000 to 350,000 persons per square kilometre*—the highest urban human concentration on Earth.
  - *Informal Economic Dynamo:* Far from being merely a residential dormitory of poverty, Dharavi functions as a decentralized industrial powerhouse with an estimated annual turnover exceeding *US \$1 Billion*. It houses over 5,000 informal micro-enterprises and 15,000 single-room factories.
  - *Key Manufacturing Clusters:* High-grade leather tanning and fashion goods; textile garment export fabrication; traditional terracotta pottery (*Kumbharwada* colony); brass metal casting; and Mumbai's primary recycling hub, sorting and processing over 80% of the metropolis's recyclable plastic scrap.
  - *Acute Sanitary Deficits:* Over 80% of residential households lack private toilets, resulting in ratios where a single shared toilet seat serves 500 to 1,000 residents in peripheral sectors. Open drains flow adjacent to ground-floor food and pottery workshops.
  - *The Redevelopment Dilemma:* High-rise rehabilitation projects risk destroying the unique mixed-use character of Dharavi, where ground-floor artisanal workshops are physically integrated with upper-floor living quarters.
]

= Module 4: Land Degradation & Wasteland Classification

== 4.1 Dynamics of Land Degradation and Desertification
Land degradation represents a temporary or permanent decline in the biological or economic productive capacity of the land, driven by accelerated soil erosion, nutrient depletion, chemical deterioration, or physical compaction.

In India, approximately *120 million hectares* (nearly 36% of the total reporting geographic area) suffer from various degrees of land degradation. Soil erosion—the detachment and transportation of topsoil by wind and running water—remains the single largest contributor to edaphic degradation.

